id: k8s.secret
kind: kubernetes
title: Secret
aliases: secret, credentials object
tags: configuration, security
questions:
  - how do you handle credentials in Kubernetes?
invariant: Secrets are configuration objects with special handling (not encryption by default) intended for sensitive values.
answer_30s: Secrets store sensitive values base64-encoded and can be mounted as files or env vars, with optional encryption at rest in etcd and RBAC-gated access.
answer_2m: |
  Base64 is encoding, not encryption: the security comes from RBAC, from etcd
  encryption at rest (EncryptionConfiguration), from not logging the object, and
  from where it lands in the Pod. Mounting as a file beats env vars because env
  vars leak through crash dumps, /proc and child processes, and because files
  can be rotated in place. The modern operational answer is external secret
  management: the External Secrets Operator or Secrets Store CSI driver pulls
  from a real vault so rotation is not a redeploy.
when_to_use:
  - tokens, passwords, TLS material, registry credentials
avoid_when:
  - non-sensitive config (ConfigMap) and any value that can be avoided entirely (workload identity)
commands:
  - kubectl get secret -A => which secret objects exist (names only, not values)
  - kubectl describe secret api-db => which keys a secret exposes without printing values
failure_modes:
  - secret committed into Git or plain-text manifests
  - secret rotated in the vault but the Pod still running on the old mounted copy
  - no etcd encryption at rest, so a backup leaks every credential
follow_ups:
  - how do you rotate a secret with zero downtime?
related:
  - k8s.configmap
  - k8s.volume
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/
